    core_synastry: `When one chart's Ceres meets another's Sun at opposition, the Ceres-bearer's need to nurture and the Sun-bearer's drive toward identity face each other across the full width of the synastry axis. What the Ceres-bearer wants to provide sits at one pole; what the Sun-bearer wants to express autonomously sits at the other. Both are entirely visible to the other. The geometry produces a bond where care and autonomy are in constant encounter across difference.`,
    behavioral_synastry: `The Ceres-bearer often becomes the person who sees what the Sun-bearer needs and wants to provide it, and the Sun-bearer often becomes the person who reminds the Ceres-bearer that autonomy is real and necessary. The two chart-bearers hold opposite ends of the same care-autonomy axis for each other. Done consciously, this produces a bond where each person witnesses what the other cannot fully see in themselves. Done unconsciously, it produces a relationship where the Ceres-bearer feels they are always the caregiver and the Sun-bearer feels they must always be independent, with both roles becoming rigid.`,
